 "purpose": "Does INT8 make EEGNet faster on microcontrollers, with and without integer SIMD and an FPU; how much do the layer order, the electrode count and pruning change; and does the CMSIS-NN integer pipeline keep the accuracy?",
 "claim_boundary": "Exploratory, planned after the test results were known. QEMU executes instructions but models no timing, so the results are instruction counts per 2-s window, not cycles, time or energy, and say nothing about memory wait states. Only the network runs on the emulated core: band-pass filtering, standardization and input quantization happen beforehand. Flash and RAM come from the linker and a painted stack on emulated boards, not from real silicon. INT8 follows the TFLite specification with min-max calibration on the model's own session-1 trials; it is not the archived ONNX INT8 graph. The frozen configuration does not change.",
